{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: observability Draft 3, exact c45ef0c23772396dcf1df598ceb69737a5d48178 (tree 535c9e3b94177460a96280fc0a0b2cfe61369462).\n\nP2 — Close the metric schema and aggregation over actual native turns (§4.1/§4.3).\nBlob 5 excludes native End.answer, although the design requires the actual native End. Blob 9 publicAnswer is a separate value and cannot replace it. Native turn.ts:94 explicitly includes answer. Preserve answer as an end state and specify its relationship to publicAnswer. The merge rule sums ALL doubles, but double 1 must always be 1 and double 6 must be 0 or 1: merging two wrote=true turns produces 2 in both fields. Without per-group multiplicity, sum(_sample_interval) also counts aggregate rows rather than their underlying turns. The single terminal merged count cannot recover counts by end/reason. Define typed merge operations, an explicit multiplicity/weight and bounded accumulator/group storage. Distinct groups may themselves exceed cap minus terminal; specify how that loss makes coverage incomplete. Extend W1 to merge multiple written turns and distinct end/reason groups, check all field domains and preserved counts, and exceed the distinct-group budget.\n\nP2 — Make every lost critical turn invalidate coverage (§4.3/§4.7).\nThe gate accounts for budget/sidecar losses but omits tainted rows, including an invalid aggregated busy row. A lost busy row can then disappear while other rows satisfy min-data and yield a false no-busy result. Account for every critical loss reason, terminal/fallback sink failure and cross-invocation accounting loss. A deferred counter in the next invocation is best effort when a process restarts, no next invocation occurs, or the sink stays failed; it cannot establish complete coverage for the current window. Specify loss-reason/kind accounting that fits the bounded terminal schema, and make W3 reject no-busy whenever busy data could have been lost. Keep sampling uncertainty qualified.\n\nP2 — Make expiry agree with the algorithm (§5).\nA ring can retain 4096 rows, but a sweep removes at most 256 expired rows before a write. After one write following a long idle period, thousands of expired rows can remain, and remain indefinitely if writes stop. That contradicts the seven-day expiry and W1's all-old-rows-expire-on-next-write commitment. Define an age-filtered read window plus bounded cleanup, or a bounded complete expiry operation; distinguish query exclusion, byte deletion and capacity eviction. Test an expired backlog greater than 256 followed by one write and no more writes, and report retained-window limits truthfully.\n\nThe trace/privacy separation, isolated administrative sidecar, deterministic health selector, validated register/session boundary, shared logical probe, partial-read columns and weighted gate/version matching are material repairs. Replace the remaining absolute hex-shape privacy sentence with the structural-validation claim already made later; close the place/end/reason unions. A logical timeout does not prove the underlying RPC stopped; keep that implementation limit explicit without claiming physical drain.\n\nCoverage: FIRST primary body FULL Root, 34745 bytes/497 LF/SHA256 b5422dea1d7af9dc0e270bf0d32b3e6c7f432cbf2ae35ed3b5c5fcf3ac85e666. Both delegated reviews and audits FULL Root; primary and U3 native hashes independently verified, U3 FULL peers (30851 bytes/404 LF/SHA256 2c0bb85e09a32246cfebfa39aea5bf6b21f1a12f3843205a607e006ec39ab533). Twelve inherited Source blob identities verified, with prior whole-body/selected-unit coverage kept distinct; native End unit independently verified. Audit: /Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-checker-state/observability-D3-root-native-audit.json. This is design review only: Source execution, tests, gate, provider/browser actions and W1–W3/calibration UNRUN. Keep the complete functionality; sequencing does not remove scope.\n","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3